# Fall Prevention Fundamentals {hideTitle}

## Fall Prevention Fundamentals

Falls from height remain one of the leading causes of serious injury in industrial and construction workplaces. Choose controls for the task before starting work.

::: figure
![Diagram of a warehouse showing fall hazard zones at elevated platforms and open floor edges](/assets/fall-hazard-zones.png)
Common fall hazard zones in a typical warehouse
:::

### When is fall protection required?

Fall protection is required whenever work takes place at or above **1.5 metres** (5 feet) from a lower level, or at any height over open machinery, water, or other hazardous surfaces. This applies to walking surfaces, platforms, scaffolding, rooftops, and floor openings.

::: note {tone=note style=outline}
The 1.5-metre threshold is a minimum. Many sites set a lower trigger. Always follow the more protective standard.
:::

### Three levels of fall control

Use the hierarchy of controls to choose the most effective protection:

1. **Elimination**, redesign the task so work at height is not required.
2. **Passive protection**, guardrails, safety nets, covers over openings.
3. **Active protection**, [personal fall arrest systems]{A harness, lanyard, and anchor point that stop a fall already in progress rather than preventing it.} (PFAS) with harness, lanyard, and anchor point.

Passive protection is always preferred over active protection because it works without any action from the worker.

---

### Quick Check: Fall Protection Threshold

::: choice At what height is fall protection required for standard platforms? {points=2 required level=3}
correct: Correct. The 1.5-metre threshold is the baseline requirement. Always check your site standard, which may be more stringent.
incorrect: The standard threshold is 1.5 metres above a lower level. Review the regulations for your jurisdiction before proceeding.
( ) 2.5 metres
feedback: 2.5 metres is above the threshold. Protection is required earlier than this.
( ) 3 metres
feedback: The requirement applies well below 3 metres.
(x) 1.5 metres
feedback: Correct. The standard threshold is 1.5 metres for most work surfaces.
( ) Only on rooftops
feedback: Fall protection applies to any elevated work surface, not only rooftops.

:::

# Personal Fall Arrest Systems {hideTitle}

## Personal Fall Arrest Systems

A personal fall arrest system (PFAS) has three components that must all be present and correctly connected before work begins.

### The three components

::: sequence {style=numbered}
:: Anchorage {level=3}
The fixed attachment point must hold a minimum of 22 kN (5,000 lbs) per attached worker. Select an anchor above the work position whenever possible to reduce free-fall distance.

:: Body harness {level=3}
A full-body harness distributes arrest forces across the thighs, pelvis, chest, and shoulders. Shoulder straps and leg loops must be snug but not restrictive. The dorsal D-ring is the only correct connection point for a fall arrest lanyard.

:: Connecting subsystem {level=3}
The lanyard, self-retracting lifeline (SRL), or energy absorber that connects the harness to the anchor. Maximum allowable free-fall is 1.8 metres. Before beginning work, calculate total fall clearance using harness height, lanyard extension, deceleration distance and safety margin.
:::

---

### Inspect before use

Inspect every PFAS component at the start of each shift. Remove any component from service if you find:

- Cuts, abrasions, or burns on webbing or stitching
- Corrosion, cracks, or deformation on hardware
- Missing or illegible labels
- A deployed energy absorber (the system has been in a fall and must be retired)

::: note {tone=warning style=shaded}
A PFAS that has arrested a fall must be immediately removed from service, even if it shows no visible damage.
:::

---

### Quick Check: PFAS Components

::: choice Select all three required components of a personal fall arrest system. {points=3 shuffle level=3}
[x] Anchorage point
feedback: Correct. The anchor is the fixed connection to structure.
[x] Full-body harness
feedback: Correct. The harness distributes arrest forces across the body.
[ ] Hard hat
feedback: A hard hat is required PPE but is not a component of the fall arrest system itself.
[x] Connecting subsystem (lanyard or SRL)
feedback: Correct. The connecting subsystem links harness to anchor and controls free-fall distance.
[ ] Safety glasses
feedback: Eye protection is separate PPE, not a fall arrest component.
:::

# Summary and Reflection {hideTitle}

## Summary and Reflection

Fall protection requires both the right equipment and the right decisions. Professional judgment and site conditions may demand more than the regulations require.

### Key takeaways

- Fall protection is required at 1.5 metres or above any hazardous surface.
- Choose passive protection (guardrails, covers) over active protection (PFAS) wherever possible.
- A PFAS has three components: anchorage, harness, and connecting subsystem. All three must be inspected before each use.
- A deployed energy absorber means the system has been in a fall. Retire it immediately.

---

